 "structured": {
  "tldr": "The loud readings are not one-sided -- a weaker rupiah at 17869 per dollar [N21] and a weaker rupee at 95.82 [N27] both lean price-pressuring on palm, and only the SE Asia drought reading leans the other way.",
  "mechanism": "## Mechanism\nPalm and soyoil clear against each other in the same food and fuel pool: when soyoil trades at a wide premium, price-sensitive buyers switch to palm, and the reverse pulls demand back to soyoil. That is the declared high-confidence link on both contracts, so a palm supply shift does not stay in Malaysia -- it re-prices the switching incentive.\n\nThree loud readings dominate. Russian sunflower oil production at 7.81 MMT [N15], the 99th percentile of its own record [N17], is declared to move palm the opposite way with a zero-to-two-quarter lag -- a substitute glut that slows palm offtake and builds stocks. A weaker rupiah [N21] pulls Indonesian export selling forward, also price-pressuring. Against them, the SE Asia drought reading at 0.16 z [N42] is declared price-supportive with a two-to-four-quarter lag, and on soyoil managed money is long 101480 contracts [N18], declared same-direction.\n\nThe price-supportive palm pattern the graph names has three of its six conditions showing -- the Pacific sea-surface reading, drought and ending stocks -- which meets its own count, but ending stocks is showing on the loose side, so I read the pattern as counted rather than in force.\n\n## The record\nPalm stocks: 2.82449 MMT [N69], up +0.1966 MMT on the month [N71], rising five straight months. Malaysian stocks-to-use for MY2025 reads 0.583039 [N7] against US soyoil at 0.0604982 [N10] -- each on its own marketing year, a dimensionless comparison only. Palm at 1,117 USD/mt [N11] sits +0.269096 sigma [N13]; soyoil at 1,638 USD/mt [N12] sits +1.21357 sigma [N14]. Argentine soyoil exports read 6.65 MMT [N30], the 97th percentile [N32] -- a loosening at the margin against the tight US sheet. Board crush is 2.6 USD/bu [N48], top decile, read through 2026-08-21.\n\nThe dated documents are thin and old for this link: the most recent is 2024-04-05, noting buyers shifted to palm in MY 2022/23 when palm ran roughly 25 percent cheaper than soyoil [E2].\n\nOver the 2020-08 to 2021-01 window the CME palm contract for February 2021 delivery settled +38.9844 % [N73]; the declared substitution relation onto CBOT soybean oil held, with the January 2021 contract at +40.1812 % [N78], and onto CBOT soybeans at +49.5877 % [N79]. The relation onto ICE canola held too: +27.78 % [N74] on the January 2021 contract, US dollars against Canadian dollars with no rate applied -- the Canadian dollar per US dollar rate moved -4.7833 % [N75] over the same window. Onto ZCE rapeseed oil it held at +17.6352 % [N76], US dollars against Chinese yuan, the yuan rate -6.2184 % [N77]. For MATIF rapeseed this window predates that board's own price history. These are five-year-old prints; they settle that the substitution edge has fired together before, nothing about today.\n\n## Where the record disagrees\nThe two chains the board puts first do not settle a direction. Running El Nino into DCE palm olein: the Pacific reading [N54] sits at the a level this page could not back percentile through 2026-03-08, declared same-direction onto drought and the readings agree; ending stocks [N66] at the a level this page could not back percentile, declared same-direction onto DCE palm olein, readings agree. Five of eight past firings moved the declared way, three did not, one published no second reading; no firing carries a price reading. No dated document reaches the drought hop.\n\nThe second chain, drought into CME palm oil, has no series at its first hop. Soyoil stocks-to-use [N24] at the a level this page could not back percentile is declared opposite onto managed-money positioning and the readings agree; positioning [N18] peaked at the a level this page could not back percentile in July 2026, now 88th, lag running to October 2026, declared same-direction onto palm and agreeing. History is one firing only. Setting the pair the far end reaches: the record does not settle palm against soyoil today -- the balance-sheet readings do, and they lean soyoil tighter.\n\nBeyond these, forty-four sequences run into the two markets; the rest are not narrated here.\n\n## Cross-commodity\nThe largest moves here are also declared on one other market, DCE palm olein: the Russian sunflower reading is declared to move it the opposite way with a zero-to-two-quarter lag at medium confidence, and the rupiah reading the opposite way at zero to one quarter. The managed-money reading is declared same-direction on thirteen other markets this estate tracks, the nearest being ICE and BMF arabica coffee.\n\n## What to watch\n- **US soyoil stocks-to-use** [N24] at 5.67 %, known 2026-09-11, past the desk's very-tight line with two marketing years falling. It acts through the high-confidence edge where a lower ratio tightens the sheet and supports price. This reads wrong if the 2026-10-09 print turns back inside that line or the run breaks.\n- **Board crush** [N48] at 2.6 USD/bu, read through 2026-08-21, top decile and past the desk's high line. Its declared lag window, counted from that reading's own date, runs 2026-08-20 to 2026-11-20. It reads wrong if the next print returns the series to the middle of its own record.\n- **Malaysian closing stocks** [N66] at 2.82 MMT, known 2026-08-01, past the desk's high line with five months rising -- the high-confidence edge says rising stocks pressure palm. Its lag window runs 2026-08-01 to 2026-11-01; next print 2026-10-10. It reads wrong if that print turns back inside the line or the run breaks.\n\nI am taking the palm stocks item in place of the nominated Pacific sea-surface item: it is the reading that sits on the palm sheet this question asks about.\n\nOn reach: the newest number behind this page was known 2026-09-21 and the oldest 2020-12-31; the newest dated document is 2024-04-05; the board price tape is not carried on this page."
